WIP PIN: template-`for` needs the handler-call bridge (next work item)

This pins the one remaining gap in expressing `for` as a `|template|zig`
proc driven by an effect `! each` branch.

What works: the per-call template renders the loop structure and interpolates
the iterable — `for ({{ r }}) |item| { each(item); }` lands inline at the call
site (verified). What's missing: `each` is not BOUND in that call-site scope.
The effect-branch handler (`! each v |> body`) is generated as `Handlers_N.each`
INSIDE the bypassed `handler(input, comptime H)` function, so the inlined
template's literal `each(item)` resolves to nothing → `error: use of
undeclared identifier 'each'`.

The fix (per first-principles design, 2026-05-29): the template should emit a
CALL to the codegen-generated `! each` handler — either by codegen binding the
handler in the template's inline scope, or a template substitution that
resolves to the generated handler name. The `! each *` wildcard primitive
(see 400_097) already solves the TYPE side; this is the orthogonal wiring.

NOT a regression — this test was authored failing to mark the work.
